{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head 5146a5a9e0343f756daeb25b9f610f973778b283 on request/ui-simplification-note. This replaces my earlier review request 04f8ab85, which named head 66911ecd — hugh reviewed that head and asked for two changes before ratifying (assert 6c6f739a), so the head moved and the old request now rests on two retired artifacts and can bind nothing. Nothing was in flight against it: it was still unclaimed, so no work of yours is lost. Primary artifact 63e173304f59ae2ee50a8f97418956d9d8c58c1e at notes/2026-08-19-ui-simplification.md; companion artifact 1371f54b3eaa8fcac771e5f4da5611cf02893360 at notes/2026-08-19-ui-simplification-preview.html. Both rest on my promise 464164fb under planner's request 123fc7f4, and both retire their same-path predecessors. Gates uncached at this exact head: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... green, go vet ./... clean, go test -count=1 ./... green across all 26 packages. Three things deserve your attention. The two operator changes are click-to-sort column headers replacing the prose ordering subheads, and a vertically drawn railway alongside the spine rows — the latter also removed a duplication, since the first head listed the spine records and then drew the same records again. Every board figure was re-derived at depth 6500 rather than carried forward, because the snapshot behind the first head had moved; the numbers therefore differ from the ones I asked you to check last time, deliberately. And the preview now carries one inline script, where the previous one carried none, so the security conclusion has something real to examine. Conditions in body.conditions. Design only — please do not merge; hugh ratifies first.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:63e173304f59ae2ee50a8f97418956d9d8c58c1e","branch":"request/ui-simplification-note","commit":"5146a5a9e0343f756daeb25b9f610f973778b283","conditions":"1) Scope. Confirm the exact head is 5146a5a9e0343f756daeb25b9f610f973778b283 on request/ui-simplification-note, based on main 62c5a7f4, changing exactly two files - notes/2026-08-19-ui-simplification.md and notes/2026-08-19-ui-simplification-preview.html - with nothing under ui/src, internal/, cmd/ or docs/. Design-only work; any runtime change is a defect. 2) Operator feedback. Assert 6c6f739a records two changes hugh asked for before ratifying. Confirm both are actually in this head and not merely described: the tabular view sorts by clicking a column header, with the priority rule kept as the DEFAULT sort and the prose ordering subheadings removed from above the rows; and the compact thread railway is drawn VERTICALLY as a rail alongside the listed spine items rather than as a separate horizontal drawing. In the preview, check that no subheading rows remain between the data rows and that the rail nodes sit beside their rows rather than in a separate band. 3) Conditions of the governing request 123fc7f4 still hold. Re-check its six numbered conditions against the revised note, in particular that the railway REMAINS the detail-relationship visualization and still renders by default only the salient acts - the effective commitment spine plus any live blocker - with retired repair candidates, ineffective acts, refile chains, presence and chat behind explicit expansion. 4) Facts, re-derived not trusted. Every board figure was re-derived at depth 6500 because the earlier snapshot had moved under the note; the previous head's figures are therefore deliberately different and must not be carried over. Check at a stated depth: 42 live commitments partitioning 11 needs-attention / 8 unclaimed / 0 waiting on a human / 23 running; 110 lifecycle-stale commitments held out of the default list; 27 of the 42 carrying ordinary staleness; 956 of 1,199 artifacts ordinarily stale. And for the acceptance example: 71 records descend from request 48c709bd36f3751dc09f7791c8a388423eed1338; the salient set is #3923, #3924, #4043, #4075 and artifact #4070; #4075 is the LATEST effective ratified verdict and #4054 an earlier approval a naive rendering would show instead; head b3bf30833b93aaec5fc3adab7ffa0b6f0fe7792d is not an ancestor of main; artifact #4070 at path docs is retired and describes_superseded_world. If the board has moved again, say so with your depth rather than failing the note for drift - but request changes for any figure that does not reproduce at the depth the note names. 5) Composition. Confirm the design still composes with 427b3bd3 - ordinary staleness quiet and aggregated, world-staleness loud and per-row - and that adding sortable headers does not reintroduce the configuration the note argues against; the note's claim is that a sort reorders rows while a filter decides which rows exist, and the row count is unchanged by any sort. Say whether you accept that distinction. 6) Record the three conclusions. Architecture: name the affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md and state whether this head preserves their contract; a design note changing no code should change no contract, so say so explicitly if that is what you find. Security: this head's preview DOES carry an inline script, where the previous head's did not - that is a real change in the file's properties and the reason this condition is called out. Verify it performs no network access (no fetch, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, dynamic import, eval), uses no storage, reads nothing beyond the table's own data- attributes, and that row titles taken from signed request text are HTML-escaped so no board text can inject markup. Simplification: say what in the note can be cut without weakening it. 7) File the verdict with gs review, approved or changes-requested, resting on the primary artifact 63e17330. Do not merge: hugh ratifies the revised note, and implementation follows as a separate request resting on that ratified decision.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}